Customer portal: line-by-line audit — remove dead code, kill duplicates, fix contradictions

Read all ~1440 lines of CustomerPortal.tsx and cross-checked every backend action.

FRONTEND (now provably clean — zero dead symbols):
- Removed Kpi(): defined at module scope, called by nothing.
- Fixed the Arabic "Request refund" confirm, which still read "only valid within
  3 working days if the supplier did not respond" while the English had already
  been corrected. Both languages now describe the notify-keeps-the-window-open flow.
- Corrected the escalation comment that claimed "one implementation of 3 working
  days only". That is false: fn_notify_supplier extends refund_deadline and three
  add_working_days variants exist. The true point (the frontend never recomputes a
  date, it renders what the server returns) is kept.

DUPLICATE ROUTE removed:
- src/app/[locale]/customer/page.tsx rendered the identical CustomerPortal as /me.
  Deleted; /customer now redirects to /me in next.config.mjs, and is dropped from
  the middleware matcher. /me is the single canonical customer URL.

BACKEND verified (nothing changed, documented):
- customer_api repo is BYTE-IDENTICAL to deployed v33 (64,290 bytes) — confirmed by
  pulling the deployed source and diffing, not by trusting mtime.
- Of its 21 actions, 14 are used by the portal, 4 only by the retired app/app-ar
  pages (analyze_quotation, close_inquiry, detect, specs), and 3 are dead everywhere
  (save_me, confirm_reveal, reveal_quote).

docs/CUSTOMER_PORTAL.md rewritten as a complete developer reference: architecture,
every route, every component and helper in the tree, every edge function and action
(marked portal/legacy/dead), every DB function/table/policy, the escalation state
machine, the money rules, the audit result, and an approval-gated plan for the
remaining backend duplication (retiring the legacy app, stripping dead actions,
consolidating the working-day functions). Those steps are irreversible and are NOT
executed here — they await one explicit go-ahead, one at a time, snapshot first.
